{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1492","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1492","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1492\",\"parent_revision\":\"dc5f35b145c04ff55758190cd702aee773d6308fda22c99a37934ad1320db2ba\",\"root_revision\":\"dc5f35b145c04ff55758190cd702aee773d6308fda22c99a37934ad1320db2ba\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe TaskBackend Protocol docstring for append_task_section (task_backend.py:201) states that appended content appears in task[\\\"body\\\"]. The LocalYamlTaskProvider implementation (local_yaml.py:480\u2013490) always writes to task[\\\"context_notes\\\"] regardless of section_name. Consumers reading task[\\\"body\\\"] to retrieve appended sections will find nothing there. This is a contract mismatch between the Protocol specification and the implementation.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPARTIALLY REFUTED \u2014 The Protocol docstring at `task_backend.py:219\u2013234` does NOT use the words `task[\\\"body\\\"]`. It says \\\"Append markdown content to a named section of a task body\\\" \u2014 which is ambiguous about the storage field. The issue description overstates the Protocol promise.\\n\\nVERIFIED \u2014 The implementation at `local_yaml.py:490\u2013529` writes to `context_notes`, not `body`. The implementation's own docstring at lines 491\u2013495 explicitly acknowledges this: \\\"Reads the current `body` field, appends the section heading and content, then writes back via `set_fields`. This satisfies the Protocol contract that content appears in `task[\\\"body\\\"]`.\\\" \u2014 but the code below (line 516) reads from `context_notes` and line 525 writes to `context-notes`. The docstring claims Protocol compliance but the code contradicts it.\\n\\nEvidence:\\n- `task_backend.py:219\u2013234` \u2014 Protocol docstring, no mention of `task[\\\"body\\\"]`\\n- `local_yaml.py:491\u2013495` \u2014 implementation docstring claims body write\\n- `local_yaml.py:516` \u2014 `existing = current_task.get(\\\"context_notes\\\", \\\"\\\")` \u2014 reads context_notes\\n- `local_yaml.py:525` \u2014 `set_fields={\\\"context-notes\\\": new_context}` \u2014 writes context_notes\\n\\n**Verdict**: The real bug is that the implementation docstring at `local_yaml.py:491\u2013495` falsely claims to write to `body`. The Protocol itself is consistent. The fix is: either (a) make the implementation actually write to `body`, or (b) correct the implementation docstring to accurately describe the `context_notes` behavior and update the Protocol docstring to match.\\n\\nGiven that `yaml_writer` does not support direct `body` writes (noted in the docstring), **option (b) is the correct fix**: align the implementation docstring with actual behavior and clarify the Protocol to specify `context_notes` as the target field for `append_task_section`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nType: defect (misleading contract \u2014 docstring claims behavior that code does not implement)\\n\\nRoot-Cause Analysis:\\n1. `yaml_writer` does not support writing to the `body` field directly\\n2. `append_task_section` was implemented using `context_notes` as the available write target\\n3. The implementation docstring was written claiming Protocol compliance without verifying it\\n4. The Protocol docstring is vague (\\\"task body\\\" not \\\"task['body']\\\"), creating ambiguity\\n5. No test asserts where appended content lands, so the mismatch was never caught\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/task_backend.py:219\u2013234` \u2014 Protocol method docstring\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/local_yaml.py:490\u2013529` \u2014 implementation with misleading docstring\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/writers/yaml_writer.py` \u2014 underlying writer (does not support body field writes)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAny consumer calling `append_task_section` then reading `task[\\\"body\\\"]` receives empty/stale body. Consumers reading `task[\\\"context_notes\\\"]` get the content. The `sam_update` MCP tool uses `append_task_section` \u2014 callers expecting body writes are silently broken. Severity: medium (data lands in wrong field, no data loss).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNone \u2014 docstring fix is self-contained. If choosing to fix implementation to write to `body`, depends on `yaml_writer` gaining body-write support first.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] The implementation docstring at `local_yaml.py:490\u2013495` accurately describes which field (`context_notes`) receives appended content\\n- [ ] The Protocol docstring at `task_backend.py:219\u2013234` explicitly states the target storage field so implementors cannot misinterpret it\\n- [ ] A test asserts that after `append_task_section`, content appears in the documented target field\\n- [ ] If the decision is to fix the implementation to write to `body` instead: `yaml_writer` is updated to support body field writes, and the docstring reflects that\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEstimate: XS (docstring-only fix) or M (if implementation is changed to write to `body`)\\nReasoning: Docstring correction touches 2 files and requires 1 new test. Implementation fix requires yaml_writer extension plus migration of existing `context_notes` data.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n```python\\nbackend = LocalYamlTaskProvider(plan_dir)\\nbackend.append_task_section(\\\"P001\\\", \\\"T1\\\", \\\"Notes\\\", \\\"some content\\\")\\ntask = backend.read_task(\\\"P001\\\", \\\"T1\\\")\\nassert \\\"some content\\\" in task.get(\\\"body\\\", \\\"\\\")  # FAILS \u2014 content is in task[\\\"context_notes\\\"]\\nassert \\\"some content\\\" in task.get(\\\"context_notes\\\", \\\"\\\")  # PASSES\\n```\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **sam taskbackend protocol docstring promises task[\\\"body\\\"] for append_task_section but implementation writes to task[\\\"context_notes\\\"]** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Quality vigilance concern from #912 \u2014 contradicted concern, real issue identified\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-05\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-24)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n**To observe the bug (documentation-level):**\\n\\n1. Read `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/local_yaml.py` lines 509\u2013513\\n2. Observe the docstring states: \\\"This satisfies the Protocol contract that content appears in `task['body']`\\\"\\n3. Read lines 534 and 543: `existing = current_task.get(\\\"context_notes\\\", \\\"\\\")` and `set_fields={\\\"context-notes\\\": new_context}`\\n4. The docstring and the code contradict each other \u2014 code writes to `context_notes`, not `body`\\n\\n**To observe the behavioral mismatch (test-level):**\\n\\n```python\\nbackend = LocalYamlTaskProvider(plan_dir)\\ncreated = backend.create_plan(\\\"P\\\", \\\"Goal\\\", [{\\\"id\\\": \\\"T1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Task\\\"}])\\nbackend.append_task_section(created[\\\"plan_id\\\"], \\\"T1\\\", \\\"Work Log\\\", \\\"Completed the refactor.\\\")\\ntask = backend.read_task(created[\\\"plan_id\\\"], \\\"T1\\\")\\nassert \\\"Completed the refactor.\\\" in task.get(\\\"body\\\", \\\"\\\")         # FAILS \u2014 body is empty\\nassert \\\"Completed the refactor.\\\" in task.get(\\\"context_notes\\\", \\\"\\\")  # PASSES\\nassert \\\"## Work Log\\\" in task.get(\\\"context_notes\\\", \\\"\\\")               # PASSES\\n```\\n\\nNote: The existing conformance test at line 319 passes `\\\"context_notes\\\"` as the section name \u2014 a YAML field name \u2014 which coincidentally works for both backends. The test does not exercise an arbitrary section label (the documented use case).\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- **Direct**: Developers reading `local_yaml.py:append_task_section` docstring believe the method writes to `body`. If they then read `task['body']` to retrieve appended sections, they find nothing. Silent data mismatch with no error.\\n- **`sam_task` MCP tool**: The `sam_update` action routes through `append_task_section`. Agents calling `sam_task(action=update, append_section=...)` expecting content in `task['body']` per the false docstring find it only in `context_notes`.\\n- **Cross-backend switching**: When an agent switches from local to GitHub backend, `append_task_section` changes target field silently (GitHub writes to `body`). #1611 tracks this broader divergence.\\n- **New implementors**: A developer implementing a 4th `TaskBackend` provider reads the `local_yaml.py` docstring as the reference implementation and may implement to the wrong contract.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `local_yaml.py:append_task_section` docstring (lines 509\u2013513) no longer claims content appears in `task['body']`; accurately states content accumulates in `context_notes`\\n2. The docstring references the reason: `yaml_writer` does not support direct `body` field writes (per `local_yaml.py:515\u2013517`)\\n3. A conformance test exercises `append_task_section` with an arbitrary `section_name` value (`\\\"Work Log\\\"`, not `\\\"context_notes\\\"`) and asserts content lands in `context_notes`\\n4. The test asserts both that the content string appears in `context_notes` AND that the heading (`## Work Log`) is present in `context_notes`\\n5. No change required to `task_backend.py` Protocol \u2014 it already documents per-backend divergence correctly at lines 220\u2013230\\n6. If the decision is made to fix the implementation to write to `body` (Option B from #1611): `yaml_writer` extended to support body writes, `local_yaml.py` and `memory.py` implementations updated, #1611 resolved concurrently\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/local_yaml.py:508\u2013547` \u2014 `append_task_section` with misleading docstring at lines 509\u2013513; actual behavior at lines 534, 543\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/task_backend.py:214\u2013253` \u2014 Protocol docstring (correct \u2014 documents per-backend divergence)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/memory.py:464\u2013490` \u2014 `InMemoryTaskProvider` (docstring correctly describes `context_notes`)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py:448\u2013455` \u2014 `GitHubTaskProvider` (writes to `body` \u2014 correctly documented in Protocol)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_task_backend_conformance.py:302\u2013324` \u2014 `test_append_task_section` (passes but uses field name as section_name \u2014 needs arbitrary-name case added)\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\nDepends on: None \u2014 docstring fix is self-contained\\nBlocks: Nothing directly; #1611 Option B implementation would supersede this fix if chosen\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nXS \u2014 docstring only (lines 509\u2013513 in `local_yaml.py` corrected + 1 new test case in `test_task_backend_conformance.py`)\\n\\nIf Option B from #1611 is chosen concurrently: M \u2014 `yaml_writer` extension + implementation change in `local_yaml.py` + `memory.py` + test updates across conformance suite\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n| File | Change | Scope |\\n|------|--------|-------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/local_yaml.py:509\u2013513` | Fix misleading docstring | Lines only \u2014 zero behavioral change |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_task_backend_conformance.py:302\u2013324` | Add test case with arbitrary section name | Test-only addition |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/task_backend.py:214\u2013253` | No change needed | Protocol already correct \u2014 documents per-backend divergence |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/memory.py:464\u2013490` | No change needed | Docstring correctly states `context_notes` |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py:448\u2013455` | No change needed | Writes to `body` \u2014 correctly documented in Protocol |\\n\\n**Blast radius: minimal.** The docstring-only fix touches 2 files and introduces 0 behavioral changes. No consumers are broken by the fix.\\n\\n### RT-ICA Snapshot (2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Goal:** Fix the misleading implementation docstring in `local_yaml.py:append_task_section` that falsely claims to write to `body`.\\n\\n| Condition | Status | Evidence |\\n|-----------|--------|----------|\\n| Protocol docstring | AVAILABLE | `task_backend.py:214\u2013253` \u2014 correct; explicitly documents `context_notes` for local/memory, `body` for GitHub |\\n| Impl docstring bug | AVAILABLE | `local_yaml.py:509\u2013513` \u2014 states \\\"This satisfies the Protocol contract that content appears in `task['body']`\\\" but code writes to `context_notes` |\\n| Code path verified | AVAILABLE | `local_yaml.py:534, 543` \u2014 `current_task.get(\\\"context_notes\\\", \\\"\\\")` and `set_fields={\\\"context-notes\\\": new_context}` |\\n| Conformance test exists | AVAILABLE | `test_task_backend_conformance.py:302\u2013324` \u2014 passes; uses `\\\"context_notes\\\"` as section_name (YAML field name, not arbitrary label) |\\n| Test gap | AVAILABLE | No test with an arbitrary section name (e.g. `\\\"Work Log\\\"`) asserts where content lands |\\n| yaml_writer body limitation | DERIVABLE | Docstring acknowledges it (`local_yaml.py:515\u2013517`): \\\"underlying query.update_plan_fields writes to context_notes rather than body\\\" |\\n| #1611 Option A/B/C decision | MISSING | Human decision required \u2014 blocks Option B (implementation fix to body) only |\\n\\n**Verdict: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS**\\n- Docstring fix (Option b) is self-contained and ready to execute with no dependencies\\n- Test gap is fixable in the same PR\\n- #1611 design decision blocks Option B only; docstring-only path is fully unblocked\\n\\n### Relationship to #1611\\n\\n#1611 (P1) tracks the broader cross-backend divergence: whether to canonicalize all backends to `context_notes` (Option A), `body` (Option B), or add a conformance-only test (Option C). The #1611 RT-ICA notes the Option A/B/C decision as MISSING \u2014 requires human input.\\n\\n#1492 (P2) is a smaller, self-contained subset: fix the false docstring in `local_yaml.py` that claims Protocol compliance it does not have.\\n\\n**These are independently workable:**\\n- #1492 docstring fix ships safely regardless of which option #1611 chooses\\n- If #1611 chooses Option B (canonicalize to `body`), the resulting `local_yaml.py` implementation change will carry a correct docstring and absorb the #1492 fix\\n- If #1611 is deferred or chooses Option C, the #1492 docstring fix is the correct minimal resolution\\n\\n**Recommended sequencing:** Ship #1492 first (XS effort, zero behavioral risk). #1611 proceeds independently. If #1611 lands Option B, #1492 is superseded and can be closed.\\n\",\"digest\":\"9697588a16f7b50d57742f73472fa1078e72cc4c26cbf1f15f32bc54ce69c531\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCPnvg\"}"}